  "title": "One Thing You Shouldn\u2019t Skip in Your Diligence Checklist",
  "date": "2024-12-20T16:02:44.103Z",
  "content": {
    "text": "96\n\n One Thing You Shouldn\u2019t Skip in Your Diligence Checklist\nHi Sara,\nWhat if I told you skipping one simple step in your diligence checklist could cause you to lose your business\u2014Too dramatic? How about missing this one thing that could cause you to go bankrupt? The world to end! - How\u2019s that for drama?\u00a0\nIn my role as a deal attorney, I am pretty much just a perpetual buzzkill when it comes to cutting corners in business deals.\u00a0\nYesterday, someone asked me if they could delete a certain section from their diligence checklist because it didn\u2019t seem relevant. I get it, it\u2019s usually in the first few lines of the list and those lists are long. As a human person and business person, I empathize a great deal with people wanting to cut down a very very long list of things to do, but unfortunately you really really should do it.\nSpoiler alert: it\u2019s very relevant.\nThe Diligence Detail You Can\u2019t Overlook\nAt the top of every diligence checklist is something that sounds boring but is absolutely critical:\nArticles of Organization, Incorporation, and Organizational Documents.\nWhy does this matter? As many people have asked me, \u201cWhy do I need this if this is an asset purchase?\u201d\nBecause these documents tell you who owns the business and what its assets are. If you don\u2019t verify ownership, you could end up in a nightmare scenario where:\nThe seller isn\u2019t actually the owner (GASP)\n\nYou paid for assets that legally belong to someone else (LOUDER GASP)\n\nAnd guess what? Whether it\u2019s a stock or an asset purchase, that\u2019s your problem to untangle after the deal closes.\nDon\u2019t Take the Seller\u2019s Word for It\nEven in an asset purchase\u2014where you\u2019re buying the stuff, not the company itself\u2014you need to know who legally owns the assets you think you\u2019re buying. These documents give you that clarity. Skipping them is a big risk and you probably don\u2019t like taking risks.\u00a0\nLet this be your friendly reminder: keep those organizational documents on your checklist. They might be \u201cboring,\u201d but they\u2019ll save you from a world of hurt later.\nNeed help making sure you\u2019re keeping your deal on track? \nClick here to check out Deal Academy, where we have courses and trainings to support you in buying and selling accounting firms.\nAnd if you\u2019re not already subscribed to my YouTube channel, where I talk mergers, acquisitions, and everything in between, now\u2019s the perfect time.\u00a0\nWeekly tips from someone in the trenches every day? Yes, please.\nTo smart and safe deals,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nBusiness Consultant and Founder of Deal Academy\n--\nFor more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers YouTube Channel videos by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2024\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
